.\" strftime.3 - format date and time
.TH STRFTIME 3 "2026-09-29" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
strftime \- format date and time
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <time.h>
.PP
.BI "size_t strftime(char *restrict " s ", size_t " max ,
.BI "                const char *restrict " format ,
.BI "                const struct tm *restrict " tm );
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR strftime ()
formats the broken-down time
.I tm
according to
.I format
and stores the result, including a terminating null byte, in the
.IR max -byte
buffer
.IR s .
Ordinary characters in
.I format
are copied unchanged; each conversion specification, a
.B %
followed by a conversion character, is replaced as below.
Names are those of the POSIX locale, the only locale Substrate has.
.TP
.B %a
Abbreviated weekday name
.RB ( Sun .. Sat ).
.TP
.B %A
Full weekday name.
.TP
.BR %b ", " %h
Abbreviated month name
.RB ( Jan .. Dec ).
.TP
.B %B
Full month name.
.TP
.B %c
Date and time, as
.BR "%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y" .
.TP
.B %C
Century (year / 100), two digits.
.TP
.B %d
Day of the month, 01\(en31.
.TP
.B %D
Same as
.BR %m/%d/%y .
.TP
.B %e
Day of the month, 1\(en31, space-padded to two characters.
.TP
.B %F
Same as
.BR %Y\-%m\-%d .
.TP
.BR %G ", " %g
ISO 8601 week-based year, with and without the century.
.TP
.B %H
Hour, 00\(en23.
.TP
.B %I
Hour, 01\(en12.
.TP
.B %j
Day of the year, 001\(en366.
.TP
.B %m
Month, 01\(en12.
.TP
.B %M
Minute, 00\(en59.
.TP
.B %n
A newline.
.TP
.BR %p ", " %P
.B AM
or
.BR PM ;
lower case for
.BR %P .
.TP
.B %r
Same as
.BR "%I:%M:%S %p" .
.TP
.B %R
Same as
.BR %H:%M .
.TP
.B %s
Seconds since the Epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC), computed with
.BR mktime (3)
from a copy of
.IR tm .
.TP
.B %S
Second, 00\(en60.
.TP
.B %t
A tab.
.TP
.B %T
Same as
.BR %H:%M:%S .
.TP
.B %u
Weekday, 1\(en7, Monday = 1.
.TP
.B %U
Week of the year, 00\(en53, weeks starting on Sunday.
.TP
.B %V
ISO 8601 week number, 01\(en53.
.TP
.B %w
Weekday, 0\(en6, Sunday = 0.
.TP
.B %W
Week of the year, 00\(en53, weeks starting on Monday.
.TP
.B %x
Date, as
.BR %m/%d/%y .
.TP
.B %X
Time, as
.BR %H:%M:%S .
.TP
.B %y
Year without the century, 00\(en99.
.TP
.B %Y
Year.
.TP
.B %z
Offset from UTC; always
.B +0000
on Substrate, which keeps wall-clock time in UTC.
.TP
.B %Z
Time zone name; always
.BR UTC .
.TP
.B %%
A literal
.BR % .
.PP
The
.B E
and
.B O
modifiers
.RB ( %Ec ", " %Oy ", ...)"
are accepted and ignored: the POSIX locale has no alternative
representations.
An unknown conversion expands to nothing.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of bytes stored in
.IR s ,
not counting the terminating null byte.
If the result, including the null byte, does not fit in
.I max
bytes, 0 is returned and the contents of
.I s
are unspecified.
A format whose result is legitimately empty also returns 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR date (1),
.BR gmtime (3),
.BR localtime (3),
.BR mktime (3),
.BR strptime (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024;
.B %s
and
.B %P
are extensions found in the BSDs and GNU.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the current time as seconds since the Epoch and as a date:
.PP
.EX
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>

int
main(void)
{
    char buf[64];
    time_t now = time(NULL);

    if (strftime(buf, sizeof(buf), "%s  %Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S",
                 gmtime(&now)) == 0)
        return 1;
    puts(buf);
    return 0;
}
.EE
